Based on the feedback the CTO has provided across several executive interviews, here is a guide for candidates on what she prioritizes and looks for in a successful candidate:
•	Hands-on Technical Depth: The CTO consistently pushes beyond high-level strategy. She is looking for leaders who remain deeply connected to the "how." She wants to see that you understand the specific AI models being used, the orchestration layers, and the code-level implementation details rather than just speaking in generalities about "agentic solutions."
How, AI. 
•	SDLC & Engineering Rigor: She prioritizes candidates who have a strong background in Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) management. She values leaders who establish engineering standards, code review practices, and rigorous MLOps deployment patterns—not just those who focus on the product roadmap.
•	Specific Domain Expertise (Healthcare & Life Sciences): She looks for a clear understanding of the regulated ecosystems she operates in. Whether it’s Epic EHR integration, HIPAA compliance, or GxP-regulated workflows, she wants to see that you aren’t just applying general AI knowledge but can apply it safely and effectively within these complex constraints.
•	Consultative & Collaborative Communication: She appreciates a consultative approach. She values leaders who can translate complex healthcare AI opportunities into secure, scalable, and production-ready architectures and who can effectively align technical solutions with client objectives. Avoid "consultancy speech" that lacks depth; be direct, specific, and clear.
•	Proof of Concept vs. Production Readiness: The CTO looks for candidates who can distinguish between a demo/POC and a real-world, production-grade deployment. Be prepared to explain how you handle scale, performance, cost-efficiency (e.g., token efficiency), and long-term maintenance rather than just creating demos to secure funding.
Advice for Candidates
1.	Be Specific About Your Stack: When discussing AI tools and models, avoid vague terms like "orchestrators." Be ready to name the specific models you’ve implemented and explain why you chose them.
2.	Demonstrate "Player-Coach" Capability: Emphasize your ability to lead teams while maintaining your ability to contribute to architecture or code patterns directly. She values leaders who have their "hands on the keyboard" to some degree.
3.	Prepare for Hard Technical Questions: Anticipate questions on specific implementation strategies—such as token optimization, guardrails for conversational flows, or how you integrate with existing EHR systems like Epic.
4.	Connect AI to Business Outcomes: While technical depth is critical, keep the business impact in mind. The CTO wants to see that you can drive workflow optimization, improve operational efficiency (e.g., 25–35% improvements), and handle the reality of clinical resistance.
5.	Be Ready to Discuss the "Why": Beyond just knowing "how" to build something, be prepared to justify your architectural choices, particularly regarding model selection (cost vs. performance) and safety guardrails in highly regulated environments.

